Local-resolver changes to BIND 9.20 (used only for the named service; see
pkgs.bind-resolver in flake.nix).

- No network: when no address family has a route, fetches wait for a routing
  change (woken by the routing socket) instead of failing at once, and a
  recursive query that still fails is dropped rather than answered SERVFAIL,
  so clients retry instead of caching the failure.
- stale-answer-ttl may be 0, so stale answers aren't cached by clients.
- Answering from the cache restarts the idle timer of open TCP connections to
  the servers the query would otherwise have gone to (tcp-reuse-timeout), so
  they stay open for reuse; useful when connections are expensive (Tor).
- prefetch-percent N: also refresh a cached RRset once it is N% through its
  original TTL, answering from the cache meanwhile.
- TCP connections to servers are only reused while idle, never shared by
  queries in flight at the same time (pipelined). Some servers reset a
  connection that receives a second query before answering the first
  (Route 53, which serves every CloudFront distribution, does), and that
  fails every query on it. With all recursion over TCP (Tor), concurrent
  A/AAAA/HTTPS lookups for one name then reliably SERVFAILed.
- fallback-forwarder ADDR [port N]: once every server for a zone has been
  tried without an answer, ask this forwarder (once) for an A, AAAA or PTR
  lookup before giving up. Recursion over Tor is TCP-only, and some zones
  cannot be reached that way: videolan.org's servers refuse TCP or are
  down. Tor's DNSPort resolves the name at the exit (over UDP if need be).
  Its answers are validated like any other; it is not used for referrals.

